{"name":"Trading Post","hex":"#bb8d3b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"trading post","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place where trading of goods takes place."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trading_post"}],"entries":[{"title":"Trading post","blocks":[{"text":"A trading post, trading station, or trading house, also known as a factory in European and colonial contexts, is an establishment or settlement where goods and services could be traded."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Trading_post_Bathrust_%28Gambia%29_1900.jpg/330px-Trading_post_Bathrust_%28Gambia%29_1900.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trading_post"},{"title":"Trading Post (newspaper)","blocks":[{"text":"The Trading Post was a classified advertisement newspaper first published in Melbourne in 1966, named for the generic concept of a trading post. After changing hands a number of times, in 2004, the company, which had grown nationally to 22 print publications and five related websites, was bought for $636 million by Telstra. In April 2009, it ceased printing and moved to an online format."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trading_Post_(newspaper)"},{"title":"Trade","blocks":[{"text":"Trade is the act by which people swap something of value to obtain another valuable item. It involves the transfer of goods and services from one person or entity (seller) to another (buyer), often in exchange for money."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Kaufmann-1568.png/330px-Kaufmann-1568.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Tradingpostguy.JPG/500px-Tradingpostguy.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wik